# Viber Project Scaffold

## What This Is

A single prompt you paste into Claude Code in any repository, blank or existing. It connects the repo to
**Viber**: it registers the project and component, wires Claude sessions to Viber's shared memory, writes a short
`CLAUDE.md` and seven slash commands, and, if the repo carries the old markdown tracking files, imports them.

This **replaces** the earlier scaffold (six agents, ten commands, and a tree of hand-written files). The old
version is kept at `docs/legacy/project-scaffold-v1.md` for reference. Nothing is tracked in markdown any more:
Viber is the single source of truth, and the files that used to carry state are rows in its database.

Viber must already be running. See `DEPLOY.md`.

---

## What Replaced What

| Before (markdown in every repo) | Now (Viber) |
|---|---|
| `BUGS.md` and the `BACKLOG.md` bug mirror | Items of kind `bug`: `viber_bug`, `/bug`, dashboard **Work** |
| `CHANGELOG.md` | Changes: `viber_quick`, `/quick`; the changelog is a generated view (`GET /v1/projects/{p}/views/changelog`) |
| `MEMORY.md` (decisions, patterns, constraints) | Rules: agents propose, **you accept** in the dashboard Review queue; accepted rules are injected into every session |
| `Implementation/Architecture/ADR.md` | Decisions: `viber_decide` records them as Proposed, you accept in the dashboard |
| `Implementation/Learnings.md` | Facts (`viber_remember`) and handoffs (`viber_handoff`) |
| `REVIEW-QUEUE.md`, `ESCALATIONS.md` | Dashboard **Review queue** (proposed rules, proposed decisions, flagged items) |
| `/load-context`, reading `Pipeline.md` and friends at session start | A hook builds a small **context pack** for each session automatically |
| Two sessions editing the same file unaware | The session registry warns on a file another active session touched |
| Hand-written session notes for the next session or a fork | `viber_handoff`: sibling and forked sessions see it at start |

**Not replaced yet:** stories, acceptance criteria, tasks, sprints, and the analyst, architect, decomposer,
product-owner and coder agents with `/spec`, `/plan-tasks`, `/plan-sprints`, `/implement-sprint` and `/flow`.
Viber's design keeps them as an optional "full lane" (`SPEC.md` section 4), but that is **not built**, so this
scaffold does not pretend otherwise. Until it is, work goes through the quick lane (`/quick`) or as items
(`/bug`). Accepted rules are not yet rendered back into `CLAUDE.md`; they reach sessions through the context pack.

